schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: Consent-based Governance (sociocracy)
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    A decision-making mode where proposals pass unless someone raises a
    reasoned objection ("can anyone live with it?"), rather than requiring
    unanimous enthusiasm or a simple majority vote.
  source_traditions:
    - sociocracy (the article names this explicitly as "the core of sociocracy")
    - >-
      ReFi DAO's Steward Council as a real-world worked example (first
      stewards election moving decision-making from founders to elected
      stewards)
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Named as the mechanism that makes the article's third governance model
    (the Council) legitimate and fast — a small elected/rotating council
    handles day-to-day decisions, big calls still go to the whole
    community.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - simple-majority voting (faster to explain, riskier for minorities — the article contrasts these directly)
    - full consensus (unanimous enthusiasm — the article frames this as "far safer... but far... slower to chase")
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      consent ≠ consensus — consent asks "can anyone live with it?"
      (objection-based, faster); consensus asks "does everyone love this?"
      (unanimity-based, slower)
  tensions:
    - >-
      the article recommends term limits/rotation as the guardrail against
      "power concentrating and the council drifting out of touch" — an
      acknowledgment that consent-based councils are not self-correcting by
      design alone
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Calling a structure "consent-based" does not guarantee it functions
    that way in practice — the article's own two guardrails (term limits,
    a written line on what the council can decide alone) suggest the label
    needs enforced structure behind it, not just adoption of the vocabulary.
  maturity: raw
  public_use: source-linked-unreviewed
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/local-node-model.md
    transformation: synthesized
    authorship: ai-assisted
